A listing in a shopping agent's cart has just sold out. Find replacements for that cart line among the other listing records shown, with these reading rules: a base or boost clock or frequency (keys such as base clock, base frequency, boost clock) does not state a processor speed or clock speed requirement, and neither does a GHz figure in the title or description of a listing whose attributes give only such a base or boost value; the listing's attribute for a property decides when its key names that same property (any key spelling, one element of a list is enough, synonyms and unit conversions count, a value containing the required value's words counts, such as "deep teal" for teal, limits include the edge); a value under a key that adds the item's own name to the property (a bag's "bag shape") counts even when the bare key ("shape") says something else; a key for a different property, even a related one (a handle colour is not the body's colour), decides nothing; when no attribute names that property, the wanted value stated under another attribute or in the title or description counts (a bare figure that does not say what it measures counts for nothing); a requirement the category label itself states is met by that category; the category label must match the item; a rule of being unlike another listing's value counts as met unless the records show that same value; out-of-stock listings and listings that break the budget do not fit.
Return ONE JSON object and nothing else: {"fits": [{"id": listing id, "price": its price as a number as the record shows it} for every listing that appears to meet every requirement of the sold-out line's item, at most 6], "reason": "one sentence"}
For a generic property, a key naming that same property and this listing category may be more specific than the generic key. A different component or predicate is not equivalent. If equally specific keys conflict, resolve from the raw evidence; do not treat a matching value alone as proof.

REQUEST:
Y'all got any jeans in a slim fit? Fair warning, I'm a skeptic till I see it (I'll double check the fit myself, don't worry), but I'm willing to be proven wrong. And I want a pair y'all actually have on hand, not one that's just listed on a website somewhere (I've chased those trails before, ended up at a dead end). Price ceiling is 1050 cts, nothing over (yes, every penny counts for me), since student loans ain't gonna pay themselves. Snag last time, y'all: the tag number matched mine, but the pair hung all wrong on me (returning them cost gas money). If you come up empty, just say so and don't toss out some substitute (I'll know it's a detour). Not climbing that switchback twice, since I'm a broke student. Total, you can send me 3 messages and no more than that. Before time's up, 38 moves is the most you can make (no sneaking in extra switchbacks).

REQUIREMENTS IN FORCE:
- item: jeans, quantity 1; requirements: none beyond the kind of product; dropped by the shopper, so any value of these now fits: fit: slim
- budget: at most 1050 cents in total
- pick: any listing that fits everything
- in stock only
- rule from the request: Spend no more than 1050 cents in total
- rule from the request: Send no more than 3 messages to the shopper
- rule from the request: Use no more than 38 steps or moves
- rule from the request: Only buy pairs available on hand now, not web-only listings
- rule from the request: If nothing fits, say so; offer no substitutes
- if nothing fits: end without naming or suggesting any listing
- listing attributes come from datasheet_for_item; a listing's page (what a chain of pages or a page check means) is shown by view_listing

